carl jung once said until we make the  unconscious conscious it will direct our  life and we will call it fate i want you  to ask yourselves for a moment how  conscious are you of your motivations  and of your drives how conscious are you  of the emotions that impact your  decisions are you aware of them or do  you have a tendency to suppress them  this year and a half of the GSB I've  learned that as a leader I have to be  able to recognize my emotions for the  potential they have to lead me to make  better decisions and to forge stronger  connections growing up my parents made a  big effort to teach my brother and me  important values I recall two of them  today the importance of being genuinely  caring and the importance of being  strong and resilient but very early on I  started to suppress emotions that  contradicted this values I started to  suppress for example fear because it  contradicted being strong and resilient  and this tendency to suppress emotions  has led me to wrong and sometimes  catastrophic decisions and I want to  share a couple with you thick not hung a  Vietnamese Buddhist leader also said  something that resonates a lot with this  value of being genuinely caring he said  the most important gift you can offer  anyone is your attention and yet I was  finding challenging for me to offer my  full attention to my friends and this  became evident last quarter when I took  touchy-feely during this three hour long  sessions I was finding it difficult to  connect with the problem my friends were  sharing I was finding myself being  so absent and distracted but my friends  in this class helped me recognize an  emotion that I was feeling all along for  some of the problems they were sharing  and this motion was contempt I was  feeling it and I was unconsciously  suppressing it because it contradicted  this value in this image image I had  about myself and I have learned from my  parents being genuinely caring but just  being able to recognize it completely  changed my experience in touchy-feely I  was no longer reactive to it but I was  being conscious of it and being able to  regulate my reactions when I felt it and  I found myself being able to pay  increased attention to my friends and I  found myself being able to build  stronger connections by being a better  listener well being a better lizard not  only helped me there it has also helped  me in the most important relationships  of my life for example with my wife was  here today with with us you can ask her  how being a better listener has been  proved a relay a relationship very much  but it's hard to tolerate and accept our  negative emotions by suppressing them we  create the fiction that we are under  control but this is not true when we  suppress them we act reactively and this  is not being in control let me share a  second episode in which suppressing an  emotion led me to make costly decisions  before coming here I was supervising  various projects at a recycling company  I had started with my brothers 10 years  before one of these projects was in a  new field for us in which we had no  previous experience and our partner on  this project started having delays  however at the moment I reasoned that I  shouldn't pay increased attention to  this project given the other important  things I have to attend to but the truth  is that wasn't this logical relation  reason that drove this decision it was  another emotion I was suppressing it was  fear I was afraid of looking deeper into  things and finding out that perhaps I  didn't have the solution the perfect  solution to this problem I was afraid of  looking deeper into things and finding  out that our partner could not deliver  which is what finally happened  but things should not have gotten to  this extreme I should have acted earlier  I should have made decisions but I was  suppressing fear and because I was  suppressing it  I was also denying the importance of  this situation I was suppressing it  because it contradicted and it  challenged my sense of strength and I  recognized this fear I'm sure I would  have acted sooner I would have acted  differently and things would not have  turned out this way but here is an  important thing at the moment when I  suppress this fear I was able to come up  with all sorts of logical reasons to  back up my decision to substantiate what  why my decision to give my attention  span unchanged was the correct one you  see we're very good at justifying our  decisions and at rationalizing  everything we do and and rationalizing  our choices as the correct and logical  ones but when we exclude our emotions  from our decision making process we deny  ourselves from the important information  they convey about us and about the  circumstances we face but fortunately  not everything in my case is bad news I  want to share a final story in which  being able to recognize my emotions led  to more positive outcomes last quarter I  also took the negotiations class with  Margaret Neel highly recommended class  but the most important learning was not  the theoretical component of the class  it was again about myself during  negotiations I had always an eagerness  to reach a rapid agreement and  in reading wanting to reach that rapid  agreement I always made fast concessions  to my counterpart and I saw myself as an  effective negotiator because I could  reach an agreement rapidly but the  reason why I was making so fast  concessions to my counterpart was not  because I was being effective but  because I was feeling anxious and  uncomfortable with perhaps the  underlying notion that I negotiation to  take something away from your  counterpart which is not true because  you you also give something to your  counterpart in both parties emerged  better than what they were and I had the  opportunity this past winter break to  test this awareness back home I have a  chance to lead an important negotiation  for my company and I anticipate  additional emotional reactions and  became comfortable with them and was  able to self-regulate and plan  accordingly  and we closed the deal in better on  better terms than we had initially  expected so the first conclusion is that  feeling fear feeling anxiousness feeling  disdain feeling contempt feeling  jealousy doesn't make you a bad leader  nor does it make you a bad person  it is our actions that define us not our  emotions but by suppressing our emotions  we can make wrong and sometimes  catastrophic decisions and take the  wrong paths and the wrong actions so  finally let me share with you ways in  which you can increase your awareness  that have been personally helpful for me  first of all if you are twin here take  advantage of the opportunities you have  the GSB take a look at the courses that  offer you opportunities to increase your  awareness some very helpful ones  personally have been of course touchy  feely high performance leadership path  to power another one across the street  called stress and awareness that teaches  mindfulness meditation which is a tool  that helps you to sit comfortable with  your current emotions second belt  personal and professional relationships  that are conducive to honest feedback  the best way to learn about yourself one  of the best ways to learn about yourself  is by letting others help by enabling  others to do so finally and most  importantly give yourself time to  reflect to take a step back and gain  deeper understanding of your emotions  and of your motivations and in light of  the initial quote to shed consciousness  to your decisions so that you can choose  your fate thank you
 

